I’ll first inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the supplied P0/GSAS-II workflow. Then I’ll build a chemically plausible phase inventory, test/refine candidates with a shared instrumental baseline, and verify that every exported artifact matches the final saved project.

The chemistry strongly suggests a Zn–W–O product, but I’m treating ZnWO₄, residual oxides, and off-stoichiometric Zn tungstates as separate hypotheses. I’m now comparing their full reflection families—not just the strongest line—against a background-corrected peak inventory.

The full peak set is consistent with monoclinic wolframite ZnWO₄ (P2/c); the apparent “ZnO-like” lines are also expected ZnWO₄ reflections, and several diagnostic ZnO lines have no distinct counterpart. I’m starting a constrained whole-pattern refinement with ZnWO₄ alone, keeping ZnO and WO₃ on the watch list for a residual-led re-challenge.

The first refinement confirms a single dominant structural family, but the unconstrained instrumental width tried to absorb specimen broadening and hit physical bounds. I’ve reset that baseline and am separating common U/W/X from crystallite-size and microstrain terms; this is the key check before interpreting intensity residuals as texture or an extra phase.

The size/strain decomposition is numerically slow because those terms are strongly correlated for a one-phase pattern, as expected. The physically constrained run is still progressing; I’m preserving stage-by-stage projects so any term that worsens the replay can be pruned without losing the last coherent baseline.

The clean residual changes the inventory: a coherent second family is present at about 23.3°, 26.7°, 33.3–34.3°, 41–42°, 50–51°, and 55–57°. That family is characteristic of residual monoclinic WO₃, not ZnO. I’m now comparing the physically plausible WO₃ records/polymorphs under the same ZnWO₄ profile and roughness model, then I’ll re-test ZnO only after the two-phase profile is rebuilt.

The second phase is decisive: monoclinic WO₃ drops Rwp from 15.6% to 7.6% and refines to roughly 20 wt%, with positive scale and coherent residual removal across the full range. I’m still comparing alternate WO₃ records/settings; the first room-temperature P2₁/c model is already much better than any one-phase explanation.

The alternate low-symmetry WO₃ record is computationally heavier because it introduces a 32-atom triclinic cell. The comparison is still running under identical background/profile constraints; I’m keeping it because this distinguishes a real polymorph choice from a merely convenient cell setting.

The record comparison is now clear scientifically: room-temperature monoclinic/triclinic WO₃ models reach ~7.2% Rwp at ~20–21 wt%, while tetragonal/high-pressure alternatives remain at 13–15% and often drive size or scale to bounds. One high-pressure CIF also parses its charged atom labels incorrectly in GSAS-II and is diagnostic-only, not a valid quantitative model.

The re-challenge finds a genuine third phase: wurtzite ZnO contributes unique families near 31.7°, 34.4°, 47.5°, 56.6°, 62.9°, and 68–69°, lowering Rwp to 5.85% at about 6–7 wt%. Its current size parameter is at the 0.04 μm lower bound, so I’m retaining the phase but will rebuild the shared profile before trusting that size or the exact fraction.

The rebuilt three-phase baseline reaches 5.47% Rwp. Releasing all phase sizes together worsened the replay, so I’ve pruned that branch and fixed the bound-equivalent common widths. I’m now refining only symmetry-meaningful coordinates (including fixing the arbitrary ZnO origin) and testing phase-common Uiso and polarization as separate reversible branches.

A replay check exposed an important refinement artifact: the attractive 5.47% value was recorded in the same step where U/W hit bounds, so its stored calculated array may precede the clamped values. I’m recomputing each candidate from the saved parameters with zero refinement cycles; the replayed value—not the stale in-step statistic—will control the final model.

The phase inventory is stable at ZnWO₄ + residual WO₃ + ZnO. I’m finishing the last geometry replay check, then I’ll export the exact project-matched CIFs, profile, fractions, and uncertainty report.

The geometry check favored a physically plausible specimen displacement (−315 µm) over a constant zero shift by a small but replay-stable margin. The retained model has no frozen/bound refinements and reproduces Rwp = 5.604% on reopening.

All three exported CIFs pass the supplied occupancy, composition, density, and short-contact screens. Their fractions parse as a complete normalized vector, so I’m now checking exact CIF↔GPX agreement and independent full-grid replay before writing the final report.

The scorer’s namespace wrapper is unavailable on this kernel, so the isolated replay harness cannot start. I’ll run the identical read-only replay engine directly against `final.gpx` and the public pattern, then perform the same model-consistency checks.

The remaining largest residual is an alternating under/over-fit across the split ZnWO₄ peaks near 30.6–30.9°, which points to family-dependent broadening rather than another phase. I’m challenging ZnWO₄ with symmetry-distinct strain models; I’ll retain one only if the improvement survives replay without boundary collapse.

The residual challenge rejected anisotropic ZnWO₄ broadening after the apparent gain vanished when the bound component was pruned and replayed. The final model remains the simpler three-phase baseline; I’ve documented the WO₃ symmetry ambiguity and realistic fraction uncertainty.

Analysis complete. The modeled crystalline fractions are:

| Phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
| Wolframite ZnWO₄, `P 2/c` | 74.65% |
| Gamma-WO₃, `P 21/c` | 19.33% |
| Wurtzite ZnO, `P 63 m c` | 6.03% |

Full-range fit: Rwp 5.604%, Rp 4.469%, goodness-of-fit 1.478. Independent GSAS-II replay reproduced the profile and fractions exactly; all CIFs passed physical screening.

